3d349fbd405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518118 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=2x,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-2z,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=-2y.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a3146e0-f24e-5488-9d01-55dfe24a4858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518118 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1),z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),$ ${f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,-1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1) & =2(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1,-1) & =-2(-1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,-1,-1) & =-2(-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=-1.02$ and $z=-1.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03) & ≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-1.02+1,-1.03+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+2(0.01)+2(-0.02)+2(-0.03) \\\\  & ≈-1+0.02-0.04-0.06 \\\\  & ≈-1.08\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518157 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,0,4)=-5,\\,{f}_{x}(3,0,4)=3,\\,{f}_{y}(3,0,4)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(3,0,4)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ estimate the value of $f(2.97,-0.02,3.98).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72ddfe60-c39c-529d-b7e5-b5ef0ab41db5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518157 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,0,4)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.97,-0.02,3.98)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.97,$ $y=-0.02,$ and $z=3.98$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.97,-0.02,3.98) & ≈-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2.97-3,-0.02,3.98-4⟩ \\\\  & =-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.03,-0.02,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-5-0.09+0.02-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.11\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518199 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518199 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518199 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518199 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518199 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf3c18cd-d64f-5eb9-99df-fc0a97727985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518199 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\, & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=(2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇\\,f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =-4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi (y-2) \\\\  & =4\\pi -4x-\\pi y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518229 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518229 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d3249d-d1cd-53bd-a1e4-e6f226addfa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518229 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22421f8f-e0e6-59b6-a0cb-85c1a6a1fe83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34086e95-3db3-5bf8-9be4-2678ffe5730a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518256 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518256 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6536adc9-26a3-559e-813d-4e2a00fc2c8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518256 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518307 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e047eab3-175f-5735-b074-16a1c776d03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489249 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
